<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf of Mannar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannar">Mannar</a> is a shallow bay of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> between south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, bounded by Adam's Bridge to the north. Its pearl oyster beds were fished from ancient times and enriched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> kingdom, whose port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korkai">Korkai</a> traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Control of the pearl fisheries was later contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna-kingdom">Jaffna Kingdom</a>, the Portuguese and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mannar.jpg" alt="Gulf of Mannar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: edited by M.Minderhoud · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locatie_Golf_van_Mannar.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
